  "result": "I added `split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]` to `shop/money.py`, with tests in a new file, `tests/test_money.py`. The full suite passes: 11 tests, all OK, run with `python3 -m unittest`. Nothing is committed.\n\n- **How it splits:** every part gets the same base amount, and the leftover cents go one each to the first parts, so `split(10, 3)` gives `[4, 3, 3]`. If `parts` is below 1 it raises `ValueError`.\n- **Negative amounts:** the module doesn't reject them anywhere else, so `split` doesn't either. `split(-10, 3)` gives `[-3, -3, -4]`: it still sums to -10, but the extra cent goes to the last part instead of the first. Say if you'd rather it raise an error or mirror the positive case.\n- **Tests:** they cover an even split, leftover cents going to the front, the result summing to `cents`, `parts == 1`, and the error for `parts` of 0 and -1.",
